      "content": "That makes sense from a statistical standpoint. To be more specific for our data science team, we're trying to build an index that identifies \"risk clusters.\" \n\nIf we wanted to avoid using race or income directly in the automated model, but still captured those same correlations you're mentioning, what kind of localized data points would be most effective? For example, would looking at things like neighborhood religious density, the presence of specific ethnic grocery stores/community centers, or even the rate of change in certain population demographics be considered mathematically sound indicators for our predictive weighting? We just want to ensure we aren't missing any high-signal variables.",
